# Multi-factor authentication

With multi-factor authentication on, users can enroll a second factor.
Once they have, every sign-in asks for it: after an email token, a
password, a [provider](/docs/auth/providers) or a
[passkey](/docs/auth/passkeys). The second factor can be:

- a code from an authenticator app (TOTP)
- a code your app delivers, such as by text message
- one of the recovery codes they were given when they enrolled

## Turning it on

```gleam
import howdy/auth/mfa

let assert Ok(config) = mfa.new("My app", mfa_key)
let identity = auth.with_mfa(identity, config)
```

The name appears in the user's authenticator app. `mfa_key` encrypts the
authenticator secrets at rest. It must be **32 random bytes, encoded as
unpadded base64url**, for example:

```sh
openssl rand -base64 32 | tr '+/' '-_' | tr -d '=\n'
```

Keep it outside the database, give every node of the app the same one, and
never lose it: without it, nobody's authenticator codes can be checked.
There is no key rotation yet.

Removing the MFA configuration later does not let enrolled users skip their
second factor. Their sign-ins fail instead.

MFA needs migration 11 of the package. Run
[the migrations](/docs/auth/migrations) before starting this version.

## Enrolling

On the starter account page, a signed-in user can set up an authenticator.
The page shows a key to type into the app; pages of your own can turn the
returned `otpauth:` URI into a QR code. Enrollment only takes effect once
the user types a code from the app.

Enrolling hands out ten recovery codes, once, and signs out every session.
Tell the user to save the codes before leaving the page. To issue a
different number, from 4 to 32:

```gleam
let assert Ok(config) = mfa.with_recovery_codes(config, 16)
```

## Signing in

When a user with a second factor signs in through a browser endpoint, the
answer is not a session. It is `202` with `{"mfa_required": true}`, and a
short-lived pending cookie. The page then asks for a code and sends it to
`POST /mfa/verify`:

```json
{"method": "totp", "code": "123456", "remember": true}
```

`method` is `totp`, `otp` for a delivered code, or `recovery`. Only then is
the session cookie set. The starter sign-in pages do this for you. A
provider sign-in that needs a second factor redirects to `/mfa` under the
path the provider routes are mounted at, such as `/auth/mfa`. Mount the
starter pages at the same path, or provide that page yourself.

Bearer endpoints answer with an `mfa_token` instead of a cookie. Send it as
`challenge` to `POST /mfa/token`, with the same `method` and `code`, to get
the access token.

A pending sign-in lasts five minutes, and cannot reach any endpoint that
needs a session. Five wrong codes within five minutes use up the account's
attempts, across every pending sign-in.

## Delivered codes

To send codes by text message, or any other channel, give MFA a function
that sends them:

```gleam
let config =
  mfa.with_delivery(config, fn(user, code) {
    sms.send(to: phone_number_for(user), text: secret.reveal(code))
  })
```

Configure it before `auth.with_mfa`. Send to a contact your app has already
verified for that user; never take the destination from the request.
`POST /mfa/send` sends a code during sign-in.

A sign-in that started with an email token cannot use a delivered code as
its second factor, since both would go to the same person's mailbox. It can
still use an authenticator or a recovery code.

## Remembered devices

Ticking **remember this device** skips the second factor on that browser
for 30 days. To change how long, and whether each use restarts the clock:

```gleam
let assert Ok(config) =
  mfa.with_device_trust(config, seconds: 604_800, renew: True)
```

The lifetime can be from five minutes to a year. The device is still asked
for its first factor every time. Security changes to the account, such as
a new password, forget every remembered device. Forgetting one stops it skipping
the second factor, but does not sign it out: revoke its sessions too.

## Managing factors

All under the API mount, and all on the starter account page:

- `GET /security` reports the current method and what is turned on.
- `POST /mfa/enroll` with `{"method":"totp"}` or `{"method":"otp"}` starts
  enrolling, and `POST /mfa/enroll/confirm` with `{challenge, code}`
  finishes it and answers the recovery codes.
- `POST /mfa/recovery` replaces the recovery codes.
- `POST /mfa/disable` turns MFA off for the account.
- `GET /mfa/devices` lists remembered devices, and
  `POST /mfa/devices/revoke` with `{id}` forgets one.

Turning MFA off and replacing recovery codes both need a recent sign-in
that passed the second factor, and both sign out every session and forget
every remembered device.

## Without HTTP

With MFA on, a sign-in can end in one of two ways. Use the `_step`
functions, which say which:

```gleam
case auth.exchange_step(identity, token, client) {
  Ok(auth.SignedIn(session)) -> todo
  Ok(auth.SecondFactor(challenge)) -> todo
  Error(error) -> todo
}
```

`auth.login_password_step` does the same for passwords. Then:

- `auth.verify_mfa(identity, challenge, auth.Totp, code, remember)` returns
  an `auth.MfaSession`: the session, and a remembered-device token when
  `remember` is `True`. The other methods are `auth.DeliveredCode` and
  `auth.RecoveryCode`.
- `auth.send_mfa_code(identity, challenge)` sends a delivered code.
- `auth.use_trusted_device` skips the code for a remembered device.

`auth.exchange` and `auth.login_password` answer `Forbidden` for a user
with a second factor, since they cannot return a session yet. Management is
`auth.begin_mfa`, `auth.confirm_mfa`, `auth.mfa_status`,
`auth.disable_mfa`, `auth.regenerate_recovery_codes`,
`auth.trusted_devices` and `auth.revoke_trusted_device`.

## Details

Authenticator codes are six digits, change every 30 seconds, and accept
the code either side of the current one. A code works once. Secrets are
encrypted with AES-256-GCM. Recovery codes are 80 bits, stored hashed, and
each works once.

A session that passed the second factor is recorded with the method
`mfa:<first method>`, such as `mfa:password`.
